A||||10||DaRkLoRD||Why the Fiero was cancelled.
Z||000000||DaRkLoRD||08-09-99||11:00 AM||||This probably isn't news for a lot of you, but I found this interesting.<p>"I recently met a retired G.M. engineer, who worked on the Fiero project and<br>he gave me an inside view of the internal politics of G.M and an explanation<br>of why the Fiero was killed. He was on the team that originally designed the<br>Fiero as quick little two seat, entry level sports car.  When the initial<br>feasability study was completed it was submitted to upper management. The<br>problem was its designation as a two seat "sports" car. There is an<br>unwritten policy within G.M, that the Corvette is "their" two seat sports<br>car and Chevrolet  "owns" that niche, within G.M.  Even though the target<br>market would have been entirely different, G.M. brass forbade production of<br>the car. The target price would have been in the 10 to 12 thousand dollar<br>range. The engineers returned to the drawing boards and presented almost the<br>same car to the G.M. brass, but this time called it a two seat<br>"commuter"car. Because it was not a "sports" car the brass agreed to finance<br>the project and study it further.  The original $12000 car had a lot of<br>unique (to the Fiero) suspension, brake and running gear components<br>including a 3.4 litre V6, as standard. When this car was submitted to the<br>bean counters, they balked at the 10 to12 thousand dollar price tag for a<br>"commuter car" and the design was rejected. The beancounters told them, if<br>the price could be cut to around $6000, it would have a chance at<br>production. Now the engineers had to find a way to cut the price of their<br>disguised "sports" car.  The special brakes and suspension pieces had to be<br>scrapped and replaced with a mix of off the shelf Cavalier parts. The V6 and<br>a heavy duty 5 speed transaxle where also dropped in favor of a 4 cylinder<br>and Isuzu produced 4 speed. After more cost cutting, and part shelf<br>engineering the car was finaly passed by the bean counters. The problem now<br>was marketing, because the car was neither fish nor foul.  Despite its mid<br>engine layout and  radical styling, it was no sports car, with the<br>emasculated four banger. As a commuter car, it was impractical, because it<br>only had two seats, and cost about the same as the Cavalier, that begat it.<br>Another problem, was because of it's radical steel cage construction and<br>bolt on plastic body panels, the car could not be built on the same line as<br>other G.M. cars. It needed a dedicated factory, whith high sales to justify<br>its existance. At first sales were good, but still lower than expected. The<br>sales would have been "acceptable" for a sports car, with a niche market,<br>but not for a commuter car, for the masses. After the first few months, the<br>cars identity crises became apparent to the public and sales started to<br>fall. As sales fell the engineers had the unenvieable job of improving the<br>car, while cutting costs at the same time. The car was steadilly but slowly<br>improved. The car eventually got a V6 (a 2.8 rather than the 3.4 that had<br>been planned for the first car. The car got improved styling and revised<br>suspension and brakes on the later GT models, but the G.M brass had had<br>enough of the money losing commuter car. It is interesting because the last<br>GT's were actually very close both in price and performance to the the car<br>the engineers wanted to build in the first place. G.M. even gave up on the<br>commuter car theme and the car was marketed as a "sporty"car. The Corvette<br>was  (and is) still the only one marketed as a true "sports" car. It makes<br>you wonder what would have happened if the engineers had been left to do<br>what they do best. The story is not that different to what G.M. did with<br>other radically designed cars like the Corvair and the Vega- Monza coupes.<br>The final versions of these cars wearn't half bad and are what they should<br>have built in the first place. Damn the bean counters!!"
Z||000001||PaUL||08-09-99||02:05 PM||||An interesting story although some of your facts make it less believable. Isuzu never produced a 4 spd trans for the fiero, they made the 5spd. The fiero has more parts in common with the X and A cars than the J. 
